  "report": {
    "verdict": "SUSPICIOUS",
    "confidence": 82,
    "narrative": "This program exhibits a classic **keylogger behavioral pattern**: it opens the raw keyboard input device `/dev/input/event0`, creates a hidden dot-file `/tmp/.klog`, then enters an **infinite loop** alternating between `sys_read` (blocking until a key is pressed) and `sys_write` (appending the raw event struct to the log file).\n\nThe program never exits — there is no `sys_exit` call. The use of a hidden filename (leading dot, `.klog`) and the `/tmp/` directory are evasion techniques commonly used by basic keyloggers to avoid casual detection.\n\nThis could be a legitimate input monitoring utility (accessibility software, macro recorder) or a malicious keylogger. The raw `/dev/input/event0` access is the primary suspicion indicator — standard applications use the X11 or Wayland input APIs, not raw device files. Only root or users in the `input` group can open this device.",
