    "concepts": [
      {
        "term": "Blocking I/O",
        "definition": "A sys_read call that suspends the process until data is available. When reading from /dev/input/event0, the process sleeps at the kernel level consuming zero CPU until a key is pressed. The OS wakes the process only when an input_event is ready.",
        "course_topic": "Module 5: I/O and Device Interaction"
      },
      {
        "term": "Linux input_event Struct",
        "definition": "A 24-byte kernel data structure returned for each input event: `struct timeval` (16 bytes for timestamp), `__u16 type` (2 bytes), `__u16 code` (2 bytes for key code), `__s32 value` (4 bytes). Reading 24 bytes per syscall captures exactly one event.",
        "course_topic": "Module 5: I/O and Device Interaction"
      },
      {
        "term": "Infinite Loop in Assembly",
        "definition": "The `jmp` instruction with a negative offset creates an unconditional backward branch. Without a conditional exit (e.g., `jz`, `jnz`), the loop runs forever. The OS will not terminate the process unless it receives a signal (SIGKILL) or the parent process exits.",
        "course_topic": "Module 2: x86-64 Instruction Set"
      },
      {
        "term": "O_CREAT | O_APPEND Flags",
        "definition": "Open flags passed in RSI to sys_open. O_CREAT (0x40) creates the file if it does not exist. O_APPEND (0x400) ensures each write is appended at the end, preventing overwriting of previously captured keystrokes. Combined value 0x241 = O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_APPEND.",
        "course_topic": "Module 3: OS Interfaces & System Calls"
      }
    ]
